---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const SITE = "https://phonepad.dynshift.com/";
const REPO = "https://github.com/MNBLabs/phonepad";
---

<Base
  title="PhonePad"
  description="PhonePad turns an Android phone into a wireless Xbox-compatible controller for Windows. What it is, who it is for, what was measured, what it does not do, and where it is going."
>
  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="meta muted">Project · Android + Windows · GPL-3.0 · Version 1.0.0, 13 September 2026</p>
    <h1>PhonePad</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An Android phone as a wireless Xbox-compatible controller for a Windows
      PC, over the Wi-Fi you already have. Nothing to buy, no Bluetooth, no
      cable.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a class="btn" href={SITE}>phonepad.dynshift.com</a>
      <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>

    <h2>The problem it solves</h2>
    <p>
      A growing share of PC gaming assumes a controller. Console ports often
      have no keyboard and mouse support; Xbox Cloud Gaming streams console
      games that cannot get past the title screen without a pad. Most people
      have a phone within reach and no controller at all. PhonePad makes the
      phone the pad.
    </p>
    <p>
      The obvious approaches do not work. Android does not let an ordinary app
      present the phone as a Bluetooth HID gamepad, and a keyboard emulator
      is not a controller as far as a game is concerned. What does work is a
      small program on the PC that creates a <em>virtual</em> Xbox 360
      controller on an XInput slot, driven by whatever the phone sends over the
      network. Windows lists it in <code>joy.cpl</code> like any other pad, and
      at the level a game looks there is no difference.
    </p>

    <h2>What it consists of</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The app</strong>, for Android, built with Flutter. The on-screen
        controller: sticks, triggers, d-pad, buttons, and a layout editor that
        lets every control be moved, resized, rotated, hidden or tuned. Four
        presets ship (Standard, Racing, Precision, Southpaw), with separate
        arrangements for landscape and portrait that switch when the phone
        rotates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The companion</strong>, for Windows, written in Rust. A single
        self-contained program with no installer. It creates the virtual pad
        through the ViGEmBus driver, receives and authenticates the phone's
        packets, and shows what is happening: round trip, packet rate, loss,
        and why anything was rejected.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The protocol</strong> between them, documented in the repository
        with a fixture both sides are tested against. Every packet is
        authenticated with a key established at pairing, so nothing else on the
        network can inject input. There is no account, no server and no
        telemetry; the phone talks to the PC and to nothing else.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What was measured</h2>
    <p>
      The figures the PhonePad site quotes were recorded on a Galaxy S24 Ultra
      and a Windows 11 PC on ordinary home Wi-Fi, and the method is in the
      repository's benchmarks document alongside the raw output.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Round trip, phone to PC and back: <strong>4.9 to 8.0 ms</strong>, typically about 5 ms.</li>
      <li>Packet rate: <strong>249 a second</strong>, matching the configured target.</li>
      <li>Loss: <strong>0.0%</strong> across 42,805 consecutive packets.</li>
      <li>Jitter: <strong>0.1 to 0.6 ms</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        The virtual controller itself adds under a tenth of a millisecond on
        average:
        a probe writes a known state and reads it back through Windows' own
        XInput API.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Two findings from that work shaped the product. The first attempt at a
      250 Hz test sender managed 65 Hz, because Windows rounds socket timeouts
      up to its 15.6 ms scheduler tick; the sender had to be rebuilt around a
      non-blocking socket. And the touch sticks were rebuilt rather than
      retuned: a physical stick needs an inner deadzone to hide spring slop and
      sensor drift, a touchscreen has neither, and the game at the far end is
      already applying its own. Stacking a second deadzone on top left a
      quarter of the thumb's travel doing nothing. PhonePad's output now starts
      just past the game's dead band instead.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A phone has one vibration motor where a pad has two, so strong and weak rumble are combined.</li>
      <li>Gyro aiming is not implemented. The protocol reserves a flag for it; nothing else exists yet.</li>
      <li>One phone at a time.</li>
      <li>
        No iOS version. There is no way to ship this on iOS that works as well
        as the Android one, and doing it badly would be worse than not doing it.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Windows companion is not code-signed, so SmartScreen warns on first
        run. Checksums for every release file are published alongside it.
      </li>
      <li>
        It relies on ViGEmBus, which is archived. It works and is what Parsec
        and DS4Windows use; the backend sits behind an interface so a successor
        can replace it without touching anything else.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it is going</h2>
    <p>
      There are no dates. In roughly the order they are likely to happen:
      per-game tuning profiles with a short calibration step, a portable format
      for sharing layouts, running the companion at login, gyro aiming, and a
      second phone for local two-player. Not planned: an account system, a
      server of any kind, or putting the controller behind a paywall.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting it</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={SITE}>PhonePad site</a> has the setup guide, documentation
      and troubleshooting. Releases are on
      <a href={`${REPO}/releases`} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>: an Android APK
      (64-bit ARM) and the Windows companion, with a SHA-256 checksum file.
      PhonePad is not on the Play Store. Bugs go to
      <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">issues</a>, questions and ideas
      to <a href={`${REPO}/discussions`} rel="noopener">discussions</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .meta {
    margin-bottom: 1.2em;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px 24px;
    margin: 1.4em 0 1em;
  }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 11px 18px;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--ground);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .btn:hover {
    background: #fff;
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
